 # Boundary Cell-Based Ray Casting
 
-Also called: BC-RC, Improved PARC
+Also called: BC-RC, Cell-Based Ray Casting, Improved PARC
 
 ## Overview
-Boundary Cell-Based Ray Casting (BC-RC) is a fast serial ray casting algorithm that accelerates volume rendering by skipping empty space using boundary cells [S1, unofficial]. It improves upon the PARC algorithm by using tiny grid cells for more accurate boundary estimation and employing fast incremental parallel projection to reduce projection overhead [S1, unofficial]. Unlike PARC, which uses pre-calculated sub-volumes whose projection is expensive when small, BC-RC projects only cells containing the object boundary [S1, unofficial].
+Boundary Cell-Based Ray Casting (BC-RC) is a fast serial ray casting algorithm that accelerates volume rendering by skipping empty space using boundary cells [S1, S2, unofficial]. It improves upon the PARC algorithm by obtaining a more accurate object boundary estimation with less computational overhead using incremental parallel projection for each boundary cell [S1, S2, unofficial]. Unlike PARC, which uses pre-calculated sub-volumes whose projection is expensive when small, BC-RC uses tiny grid cells for more accurate boundary estimation and employs fast incremental parallel projection with templates to reduce projection overhead [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
+The method consists of three main phases: offline boundary cell detection, online boundary cell projection, and ray traversal [S1, S2, unofficial].
+
+### Inputs and Outputs
 **Inputs:**
-- A regular grid of volume elements (voxels) with scalar values [S1, unofficial].
-- Camera parameters defining parallel projection geometry and image dimensions [S1, unofficial].
+*   Volumetric data set: A regular grid of volume elements (voxels) with scalar values [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   Camera parameters: View direction and image plane geometry for parallel projection [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
 **Outputs:**
-- A rendered image with ray casting optimizations (adaptive sampling, early termination) [S1, unofficial].
+*   Rendered image with composited volume data [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
-**Data Structures:**
-1.  **Volume Grid:** Regular grid of voxels [S1, unofficial].
-2.  **Boundary Flags:** A binary flag $F$ for each cell $C(i, j, k)$ [S1, unofficial].
-    -   $F=1$: The cell contains the object boundary [S1, unofficial].
-    -   $F=0$: The cell is empty or fully inside/outside (not a boundary cell) [S1, unofficial].
-3.  **Z-Buffers:**
